From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: idiazraz <100824525+idiazraz@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 17:00:01 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] mt7915: offload MT7916 RX to the EN7523 NPU

When the MT7916's device tree node links an EN7523 NPU ("airoha,npu"),
the NPU takes the MAIN and BAND1 RX rings (MT7916_D DBDC model), as
the vendor driver does, and WED is turned on with it. Other boards
keep both off. There is no module parameter.

- The rings are handed over in mt7915_dma_init, before RX DMA starts.
  Swapping a running ring leaves the WFDMA on its prefetched host
  descriptors and RX stalls.
- The NPU firmware only runs with both links of a dual-link card
  (port type P0_P1), so hif2 is kept when WED is attached. Band1 is
  addressed through the BAR of its own link.
- The host reads 802.3 frames from the NPU's WLAN RX rings 0/1 (16-byte
  V1 descriptors).
- V1 firmware only queues a data frame to the PPE when a BA node is
  active for it, and no BA session is registered with the NPU, so its
  no-BA mode (set ID 10) is set.
- EN7523 does not learn flows by itself. The NPU returns unbound frames
  hashed with CPU reason FOE_UNHIT, so the pending flow is committed on
  FOE_UNHIT and HIT_UNBIND too, through airoha_ppe_dev_check_skb as the
  ethernet RX path does.
- The NPU drops the TX status records, so a status that times out
  counts as acked.

Needs the airoha/kernel changes that match the EN7523 NPU compatible,
fix the V1 WLAN set IDs and leave QDMA0 TX ring 6 to the NPU.

Tested on a Skyworth GN630V (EN7523 + MT7916), iPhone on 5 GHz, routed
to the WAN with flow_offloading_hw: 970 Mbps down / 826-859 up. With
WED alone (uploads on the CPU) it was 962 / 382.

Limits: no RX reordering for frames the NPU sends to the PPE, frames
split over several buffers are dropped, and a DMA reset points the card
back at the host rings.
